WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.380
- So Ben, as you know, is
a world renowned expert

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.000
on the crimes committed
by the Khmer Rouge.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.610
He's the A. Whitney Griswold
Professor of History

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.610
and Founding Director of
the Genocide Studies Program

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.563
here at Yale.

00:00:18.563 --> 00:00:20.760
He's the author of the classics.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.820
How Pol Pot Came to Power.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.110
The Pol Pot Regime, Blood and Soil,

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.630
Genocide and Resistance in Southeast Asia.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.350
And the important recent book,

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:33.193
Viet Nam: A History From
Earliest Times to the Present.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.100
I think one of the most important
distinctions that Ben has

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.910
is if you wanna get his books for cheap,

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.630
you just go to Pham Ngu Lao in Saigon,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.193
and they're all on
mimeographed and available

00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:47.450
in very expert reproductions, right?

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.380
So sorry, Yale University Press.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.380
But it's kind of a mark of
having made it in the world

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.103
when people are bootlegging
your books, I think.

00:00:56.103 --> 00:00:57.790
I haven't reached that fame yet,

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.190
but I'm looking forward to
someday learning from Ben,

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.640
how to get that done.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.280
Anyhow and Eve Zucker has
been a visiting professor

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.270
in anthropology here at Yale.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.103
She's a member of

00:01:09.103 --> 00:01:12.080
the Steering Committee for the
Yale Genocide Studies Program

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.450
and current president of the
Center for Khmer Studies.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.283
Her books include

00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:18.760
Forest of Struggle:
Moralities of Remembrance

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.320
in Upland Cambodia.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.940
Mass Violence and Memory
in the Digital Age,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.980
and Coexistence in the
Aftermath of Mass Violence.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:26.850
And of course the book

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.700
we'll be talking about today as well.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.590
It's not always the case that historians

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.720
and anthropologists collaborate well,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.730
but this is a really exciting example

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.150
where the convergence
of an important topic

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.860
has brought together Ben and Eve

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.997
and also many of our students here at Yale

00:01:42.997 --> 00:01:44.760
and other scholars around the world

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.580
to come together for this important book.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.390
So I wanna say one last thing is this book

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.780
is a result of a conference
and workshop put on

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.540
here at Yale in November of 2018.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.750
So I just wanna thank them for bringing

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.930
so many important people to
do work on this topic here.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.300
So please join me in welcoming
Ben Kiernan and Eve Zucker

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.005
to talk about the book.

00:02:07.005 --> 00:02:10.005
(audience clapping)

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.037
- We would like to thank
Erik Harms and Kris Mooseker

00:02:14.037 --> 00:02:17.100
and the Council of Southeast Asian Studies

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.100
and all of you for being here today,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.400
both online and in person,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.993
which is pretty exciting.

00:02:24.284 --> 00:02:26.990
And we also would like
to thank the contributors

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.070
to the book who are...

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.850
Some of them are here
today, but not all of them

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.600
who made this project what it is.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.173
So I'm just gonna go move
over to start the PowerPoint.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:40.707
I'm gonna (indistinct).

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.000
So what is the book about?

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
the book is a thematic
and historical examination

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:59.180
of the causes and the
consequences of violence

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.100
in Southeast Asia.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.100
Particularly between the
periods of 1945, up to 2020,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.620
when the book was published.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.520
And covers six cases,
Cambodia, Thailand, Indonesia

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.530
Myanmar, The Philippines and Vietnam.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.130
And as you can see here,

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.840
we had 18 contributing
authors to the volume.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.270
Which are listed here.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.700
There's quite a few of them.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.343
So we thought we'd just list them for you.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.430
And we'll be talking a little
bit more about the book,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.300
but I just wanna move
ahead a little bit now

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.030
to the next...

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:40.293
To talk about how the book started.

00:03:42.097 --> 00:03:46.140
So this project really started
from a few different reasons.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.340
One of them was that Ben
has been teaching courses

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.590
on Southeast Asia forever.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.463
Maybe not forever, but for a long time.

00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:56.670
And I was teaching
courses on Southeast Asia

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.300
and particularly on
violence in Southeast Asia.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.400
And one of the things that we noticed...

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.310
Sorry, can everyone understand me online?

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:08.740
Is it clear?

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:09.573
Okay.

00:04:09.573 --> 00:04:13.120
So one of the things
that we noticed was that

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.830
there's not a lot of
anthologies that cover violence

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.740
of the region in Southeast Asia.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.833
The ones that do exist many
are like 10 years old or so.

00:04:25.924 --> 00:04:28.910
And a lot of them aren't
specifically about mass violence,

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.790
some focused just on the Cold War.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:32.610
Which that's a fairly recent one,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.940
but other ones also focus on terrorism.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:38.633
Like the one by Damien Kingsbury.

00:04:40.443 --> 00:04:41.276
And there's some good ones,

00:04:41.276 --> 00:04:44.720
but they'll often only
included a few countries.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.060
So we thought there was
a need for something.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.100
For something that covered
the region as a whole

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.993
on this subject.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:54.970
So that was one thing.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:56.460
Another thing that we noticed is that

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.490
there's a lot of new scholarship going on.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.120
And not only new scholarship
with new scholars

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.280
and young and upcoming scholars,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.420
but also very new topics that
really haven't been covered

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.670
before in the past.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.120
And that was really exciting.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.240
So that was another
impetus for the project.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:17.710
And the other major thing
that was going on at the time,

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.750
which I'll go over it a little
more detail in just a minute

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.820
was what was going on in
the world at the time.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.550
If we think about it, it's
a little different now,

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.920
but back in 2018, as crazy as it is now,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.820
it was pretty crazy then.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:37.340
So you have this
authoritarianism on the rise,

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:39.530
you have populism on the rise,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.730
and this was pretty
much all over the world.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.020
You know, we had Trump,
you had Putin, you had Xi

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.310
and so forth.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.350
But in Southeast Asia,

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.910
you also had the same thing happening,

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.980
particularly with Duterte
in the Philippines,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.393
and also Hun Sen.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.060
And there was also a lot
of impunity of leaders.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.010
They were pretty much
doing what they wanted,

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:07.170
with no consequences at the time.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.950
And a lot of intolerance for difference.

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:13.130
Different ideas, different
types of people and so forth.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:17.443
So to Zoom in a little bit,
and to get a snapshot of that.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:22.230
We see that during that
period you had in Myanmar,

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.140
the Rohingya Genocide taking place,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.453
which had been ongoing since 2016.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.610
You also had the tens
of thousands of Rohingya

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:40.610
being murdered in their
villages which were being burned

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.600
and other mass executions and so forth.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.293
And by 2018, there were
also 730,000 refugees

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:56.210
already in Bangladesh who
were fleeing for their lives.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.420
A lot of them were also
fleeing not to Bangladesh,

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:02.070
but also out on boats
and so forth to Malaysia.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:05.383
But it was a real crisis taking place.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:10.883
The crisis continues, but it
was pretty severe then as well.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.700
You had mass imprisonments
in the concentration camps

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.280
of the Rohingya.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.540
And finally in August of 2018,

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:26.140
you had the UNHCR declare
that the generals of the army

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:30.370
should actually be held
accountable for genocide.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.130
So that was Myanmar.

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:33.273
A little bit about it.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:39.810
In Thailand, you had two
coups over a very short time.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.530
You had demonstrations taking place.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.000
Which had been ongoing for a while.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:47.833
You had again...

00:07:47.833 --> 00:07:50.310
You see the impunity of the leaders.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.280
There's violence in the south

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.880
there's violence against
political opponents.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.780
There's surveillance happening
online, and Facebook.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.650
Also though people didn't
feel safe in other places

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.060
about what they said.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:05.513
There was numerous arrests.

00:08:08.285 --> 00:08:11.130
Sometimes for as long as up to 10 years

00:08:12.101 --> 00:08:16.193
for crimes, like saying
something about the monarchy.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.830
Turning now to Cambodia,

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:23.190
you had the national election

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.490
that took place that year in July.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.520
And that was another volatile situation.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.520
You had a lot of discomfort at the time

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.180
amongst the population who feared

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:42.610
that there were being surveyed.

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:44.103
Even in coffee shops.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.190
There was surveillance also online.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:49.760
And there were good reasons
for them to be worried.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.760
Because in 2016, the activist
Kem Lay was assassinated.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.980
And then you also had
the opposition leader

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:05.350
right before the elections,
Kem Sokha he was arrested

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:10.000
and several other
members of the opposition

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.663
were not allowed to
participate in elections.

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.680
Also in Cambodia that same year,

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:20.680
you had Case 02 happening
of the Khmer Rouge Tribunal.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.123
That was with Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.320
And on November of that year,

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.270
which was actually a week
after our conference,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:35.449
the ECC found Nuon Chea guilty of genocide

00:09:35.449 --> 00:09:39.200
against both ethnic
Chams and the Vietnamese.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.370
And Khieu Samphan guilty of genocide

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:43.850
against the Vietnamese.

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:47.120
And both guilty of
crimes against humanity,

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:49.830
including of extermination.

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:54.460
So that was a huge deal at the time.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.510
That they actually passed down
those sentences of genocide.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:00.073
And also those crimes against humanity.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:05.130
In the Philippines, the
war on drugs continued

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:07.200
and showed no sign of letting up.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.500
In fact, Duterte stated
that he had no intentions

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.253
of slowing that down or stopping it.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.590
The same year you had Derek Duterte

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:21.890
pull out of the International
Criminal Court, the ICC.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:25.810
In a blatant statement of disregard

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.033
for any of its principles.

00:10:29.262 --> 00:10:31.440
And that was the year also Maria Ressa,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:33.750
who recently won the Nobel Prize.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:38.750
Who is also the founder of
the news outlet Rappler.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:43.293
She was arrested also during that time.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:48.400
And so with all of that happening,

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.010
just in that one year,

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.220
that was the context

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:54.003
where we decided to have the conference.

00:10:55.133 --> 00:10:57.310
And that was what it
was on everybody's mind

00:10:57.310 --> 00:10:58.820
when we had the conference

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.820
was this keen awareness of
the atmosphere in the world

00:11:05.447 --> 00:11:07.290
and what was happening.

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:09.270
And so the keynote for that conference,

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:12.920
we had Jeffrey Robinson
who spoke about Indonesia.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:14.850
That was on the first evening.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:18.760
We had 24 participants in all

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.990
representing several different countries.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:24.570
And we also, at the end,

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:29.570
we had Al McCoy gave a
wonderful talk at the end,

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.230
which wrapped up all the papers.

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:35.820
And he provided some great insights

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.530
into how the different
pieces come together.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.117
And that was on the last day.

00:11:43.117 --> 00:11:46.130
And we appreciate the support of course,

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:49.150
that we got from the council
of Southeast Asian studies

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.240
and the Yale Genocide Studies Program

00:11:53.248 --> 00:11:57.010
and other parts of the
Yale MacMillan Center

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:02.010
and so forth for providing us sponsorship

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:03.130
for that conference.

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:06.150
And also of course, the New
York Southeast Asia Network,

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.823
which was our primary
funder for that event.

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:13.480
So with this, this conference

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:18.480
was really the basis for the
volume and was the papers

00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:23.150
that came out of it that
were presented on that day.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:28.150
So just going now for
a moment to the context

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:30.883
of where our book starts,

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:36.060
and that would be just
in the post-World War II.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:38.090
Immediate post World War II.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:43.090
So in 1945, on August
6th, you had a US aircraft

00:12:45.911 --> 00:12:50.590
a B-29 drop the atomic bomb onto Hiroshima

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:53.630
thereby ending the war.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:58.380
And also basically signaling
the start of the Cold War

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.350
and a new world order.

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:03.390
And so this ends up having
immense consequences

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:06.250
for Southeast Asia and the world.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.390
But in Southeast Asia,

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:10.200
because it meant that Japanese withdrawal

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:12.070
from Southeast Asia.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:15.080
And so the end of all the brutality

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.480
that they brought with
them to Southeast Asia.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:21.980
But it also left a little
bit of a vacuum there

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:23.460
for a brief period of time.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.110
The colonial powers were fun.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:27.760
Some of them would end up coming back.

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:31.520
But there was an opportunity
there for things to change

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:32.920
and for things to happen.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:37.130
It was one of those chaotic
periods that Alfred Lau

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:41.120
called a meltdown essentially.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.190
That was the context.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:48.190
And so what happened then?

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.400
So there was this
opportunity for independence

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.300
that was there.

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:56.470
And with that opportunity
for independence,

00:13:56.470 --> 00:14:00.510
then nations had to start
imagining what type of nation

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.050
would they want to have.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.540
And so now there were
these different systems

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.110
and different ideologies

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.890
that they could draw
influence from potentially.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:13.870
There was communism coming from
China and the Soviet Union,

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:17.380
and you had also American capitalism,

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:21.200
which provided another
thought form of thinking

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.580
and form of organization.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.353
So this was the context.

00:14:25.353 --> 00:14:28.700
And I'm gonna stop right
there and turn it over to Ben

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.430
who's gonna talk about the connections,

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:32.110
the historical connections

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:33.660
between these different countries.

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.390
And then later we'll come back

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:37.340
and I'll talk about some of the things.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.697
- Thank you very much Eve.

00:14:43.697 --> 00:14:44.970
I'd also like to thank

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:47.017
the Council on Southeast Asia Studies.

00:14:47.017 --> 00:14:47.850
(Ben coughs)

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:48.683
Excuse me.

00:14:48.683 --> 00:14:50.750
As well as the MacMillan Center

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.610
and the Genocide Studies Program.

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:57.264
And the Orvile Schell
Center at the law school

00:14:57.264 --> 00:15:01.440
for the support for this project,

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:05.170
which has been a pleasure to work with Eve

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:09.570
on the final book project,
as well as the conference.

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:13.950
As Eve said, she zoomed in
on the actual events of 2018

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.810
when the conference was
held and the actual coverage

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:23.270
of the various topics in the book.

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:26.400
I'd like to zoom back
out again temporarily,

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.110
just for a while and look

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:33.087
at not so much at the content of the book,

00:15:33.087 --> 00:15:37.840
but the context of the events
that we cover in the book.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.840
The much larger context,

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.660
looking back at the early
modern period in Southeast Asia.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:49.660
And some of the bigger themes
of violence in Southeast Asia,

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:53.100
as well as the chronological context

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.440
and the thematic...

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.540
Not just thematic, but chronological,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:02.540
and the causation of mass
violence in Southeast Asia,

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:07.080
I've recently had an exchange in print

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:12.030
with Yehuda Bauer, the eminent
historian of the Holocaust

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.480
in which he looked back at his work

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:19.060
over many, many decades on the Holocaust.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:20.750
And he thought about

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:23.930
the presence of the
Jewish people in Europe

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:25.670
and the importance,

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:28.440
maybe he thought about the importance

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.430
of the issue of migration

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:35.860
as a long-term issue in the history

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:37.990
of the fate of the Jewish people.

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:42.720
And I thought about
that in our discussions.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:45.340
And what can that tell us

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:49.610
about the history of mass
violence in Southeast Asia?

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:54.010
Because Southeast Asia,
unlike China and India

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:57.723
is a predominantly maritime world region.

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:02.120
Like Mediterranean Europe
and Asian Mediterranean,

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:07.120
perhaps comprising, large
archipelagos and long coastlines.

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:08.860
And as many of you know,

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:12.660
the leading historians of
the Southeast Asian region

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.290
consider Southeast Asia
to be a crossroads.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:19.760
Anthony Reid subtitled, his 2015

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:23.853
History of Southeast
Asia: Critical Crossroads.

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:29.880
And in his 1971 book,
Vietnam and the Chinese Model

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:32.337
Alexander Woodside wrote

00:17:32.337 --> 00:17:33.990
"If mainland Southeast Asia

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:36.260
was a crossroad of civilizations,

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:41.260
'the Europe of Asia' in its juxtaposition

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:44.480
of competing cultures and societies.

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:48.500
Traditional Vietnam was a
crossroad within this crossroad.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:51.373
Especially after it has
seized the Mekong Delta.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:55.857
The sum of all the political
difficulties," he concludes.

00:17:55.857 --> 00:17:59.647
"And the cultural
tensions was tremendous."

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:05.620
Now Southeast Asia's earliest
documented cases of genocide

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:09.480
involved ethnic groups
migrating along the coast

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:12.300
or from the interior to the coast

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:15.300
or encountering new religious movements

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:18.490
arriving in the region by sea.

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:21.910
Along the Eastern coast of
mainland Southeast Asia,

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:24.300
the Vietnamese southward expansion

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:29.217
and the genocide of Chams in 1471 to 1509

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:34.780
were followed by Cambodian
massacres of ethnic Vietnamese

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.640
who had subsequently
migrated down the coast

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:39.780
into the Mekong Delta.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:43.310
French missionaries reported
that the Cambodian attack

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:48.170
on the Vietnamese control
of Ha Tinh in 1731,

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:51.120
had "made an enormous carnage of them.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.600
They counted more than
10,000 of them lost."

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:57.090
Afterwards, "They killed
all those Vietnamese

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:00.750
that they found in Cambodia,
men, women, and children."

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:05.600
Then in 1750 Cambodia's Monarch
gave orders or permission,

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:07.030
the missionaries wrote,

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:09.670
to massacre all the
Vietnamese who could be found.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:13.003
And this order was executed
very precisely and very cruely.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:16.350
This massacre lasted a month and a half,

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.490
only about 20 women and
children were spared.

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:23.043
The massacre was general
from Kahlon to Ha Tinh.

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:26.540
The Vietnamese kingdom in turn

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:29.584
resorted to similar measures in the 1830s,

00:19:29.584 --> 00:19:33.080
when it conducted systematic
massacres of Cambodians

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:36.023
in an attempt to colonize their country.

00:19:36.910 --> 00:19:39.708
In Southeast Asia's Western mainland,

00:19:39.708 --> 00:19:42.290
Burmans expanded south to the coast.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:44.950
Then the home of the Mon people.

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:48.320
In 1740 Mons killed 7,000 Burmans

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:53.210
in a genocidal massacre
in the Port of Syria.

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:56.710
In 1757, conquering Burman forces

00:19:56.710 --> 00:20:00.203
massacred thousands of
Mons in their port of Pegu.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:04.900
And then on the island of Java in 1648,

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.290
the ruler of the kingdom of Matara

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:10.320
slaughtered 5,000 Islamic teachers

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:12.243
and members of their families.

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:17.070
Now despite sharing
some common (indistinct)

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:20.100
historical features like migration,

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.930
these cases, nevertheless
proved to be scattered.

00:20:23.930 --> 00:20:28.230
Relatively infrequent
outbreaks over five centuries

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:30.590
of Southeast Asian history.

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:32.680
Otherwise, I think it's still true to say

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:34.340
the region is indeed

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:37.890
a cosmopolitan crossroads
of civilizations.

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:41.640
Remarkable for harboring nearly
all the world's religions,

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:45.860
Budhism, Hinduism, Islam, Christianity,

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:50.150
and Confucianism are all
prominent in Southeast Asia.

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:53.840
So we have a major theme of migration,

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:58.840
perhaps that underlies
outbreaks of mass violence.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:02.440
But these are not really
connected to one another.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:06.140
They're scattered outbreaks
over a long period of time.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:09.230
But there are some perhaps big themes.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:11.730
What we have by contrast

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:16.290
in the cases that we look at in this book,

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:18.763
in the post 1945 period,

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:23.990
much closer connections between the cases.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:28.990
Not just big themes, but
closely related cases.

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.550
And that's what I want to look at now.

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:36.280
To Zoom back in at the
cases that we cover.

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:39.850
Particularly in the post-war period.

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:43.137
Katie Munyan from the
Schell Center, formerly of

00:21:43.137 --> 00:21:45.420
the Schell Center for
International Human Rights.

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:48.360
And the law school has a
very interesting chapter

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:52.390
on intent as a element of genocide.

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:54.350
She starts off with the legal explanation

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:59.350
of what intent means as
a component of genocide.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.250
Not just general intent,

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:05.540
meaning the action must be
deliberate, not accidental.

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:07.780
But also specific intent,

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:12.780
meaning the importance that
the action must be done

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:16.320
in order to destroy a group as such.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:20.800
It must be carried out
consciously desiring

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:23.550
the distraction of the group as such.

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:25.430
That's very important with genocide.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:28.450
Not necessarily with
crimes against humanity,

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:32.730
such as extermination, or even
(indistinct) mass violence,

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:37.040
or even let alone political
violence, which we also cover.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:42.040
But she does say that
these cases of intent

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:46.900
can be seen from a distance

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:50.890
and they could be judged as coming from

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:54.710
by nonjudicial observers.

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:57.640
And she points out that in
a number of these cases,

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:00.690
intent was pretty clear.

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:05.690
Even though it was not
accurate to say from a distance

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:09.620
or while the cases were taking place,

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:14.590
that the perpetrators were
already criminally liable.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:16.970
Because it hadn't been judged by a court.

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:21.970
But a nonjudicial authority
or observer or NGO

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.460
studying these events could state

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:28.540
that the intent was observable

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:31.593
and could have said much
earlier than they did

00:23:31.593 --> 00:23:35.020
that there was a case for genocide,

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:38.560
let alone other examples
of crimes against humanity

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:41.640
like extermination or political violence.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:43.050
And she's critical.

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:46.510
I think quite accurately
of many of the observers

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:50.170
for being hesitant in pointing out

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:53.960
where there was a case
for an investigation

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:55.810
or a criminal trial.

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:59.100
Which would decide whether
or not these perpetrators

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.240
were criminally liable.

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:03.910
And this is one feature
I think, of these cases

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:10.060
that connect a number of
the cases that we looked at.

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:13.750
That there was a delay in the suppression

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:18.530
or the intervention in these
cases in Southeast Asia.

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:21.000
I'll just give you one
example of where I think

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:24.380
there could have been
much quicker recognition

00:24:26.217 --> 00:24:27.923
of the possible intent.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:34.080
Eve has mentioned Nuon
Chea and Khieu Samphan

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:38.230
who were found guilty in
2018 of genocide in Cambodia.

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:41.700
Nuon Chea was found guilty of the genocide

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:46.183
of the Cham Muslim minority in Cambodia.

00:24:47.856 --> 00:24:48.820
And the Pol Pot regime

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:53.080
of which he was the
number two was in power

00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:55.860
from 1975 to 1979.

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:59.240
The only foreign head of
state to visit Cambodia

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:03.300
during that period, when the
Pol Pot regime was in power,

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:06.783
was Ne Win, the military
dictator of Burma.

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:11.520
He visited in November, 1977,

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:14.980
just after Pol Pot's
triumphant visit to China

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:17.840
in which he came out as the real leader

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:21.350
of the Democratic Kampuchea,
the Pol Pot Regime.

00:25:21.350 --> 00:25:25.543
He had just returned back
to Cambodia, triumphantly.

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:31.500
He didn't meet with Ne Win publicly.

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:35.950
We don't know whether they
actually met privately.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:38.570
But Ne Win, the only foreign head of state

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.490
to visit Democratic
Kampuchea in the four years

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:42.450
it was in power.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:46.410
He met four times with Nuon Chea.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:51.180
He met four times with Khieu
Samphan, the two officials,

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.380
the head of state of
Cambodia Khieu Samphan.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:56.800
If he didn't meet with Pol Pot,

00:25:56.800 --> 00:26:00.480
perhaps Pol Pot was too
busy unlocking his suitcases

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:01.930
after coming back from China.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.300
He probably had a lot
of (indistinct) suits

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:05.610
that he had to unpack.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.470
But he met four times with Nuon Chea

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:10.440
and four times with Khieu Samphan.

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:13.100
If he didn't meet secretly with Pol Pot,

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:14.710
I'll be a monkey's uncle.

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:19.010
But anyway the genocide
of the Cham Muslims

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:21.681
was well underway when Nuon Chea

00:26:21.681 --> 00:26:23.540
was later found guilty of it.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:27.970
Nuon Chea in November,
1977, returned to Burma.

00:26:27.970 --> 00:26:32.970
And in February of 1978, just weeks later,

00:26:33.536 --> 00:26:35.340
a couple of months later.

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:39.060
Operation Naga Min in Burma.

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:43.220
Which was a massive
discrimination and persecution

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:45.160
of the Rohingya Muslims.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:49.140
The first of its kind in Burma.

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:54.140
By may of 1978, 250,000,
I believe, Rohingya's

00:26:55.390 --> 00:27:00.390
had fled into Bangladesh, and
many of them had been killed

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:03.800
while they were fleeing

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:07.270
by the Ne Win military dictatorship.

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:10.000
If Ne Win had not discussed

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.410
with Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:16.090
the persecution of the
Cham Muslims in Cambodia,

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:17.390
just weeks before.

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:19.320
Again I'll be a monkey's uncle,

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:22.700
in the four meetings that
he had with Nuon Chea.

00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:27.250
Then again, of course, in 1991 and '92,

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:29.510
a second campaign began in Burma.

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:34.510
Of persecution of the Rohingya Muslims,

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:39.620
and then two more waves
ending in genocide in 2018.

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:44.620
But it wasn't until much
later that foreign observers

00:27:44.860 --> 00:27:49.490
began to call for
investigation of genocide

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:53.350
against the Rohingyas
in Burma, decades later.

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:57.410
Really the Katie Munyan
has this all documented.

00:27:57.410 --> 00:28:01.950
How hesitant international observers were

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:06.950
to identify this as an
ongoing potential genocide.

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:12.380
There was talk of ethnic cleansing,

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:15.320
which is not an international crime,

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:20.320
but gained recognition in
the Balkan case in the 1990s.

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:25.150
There were all kinds of other terms

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.690
applied to what was
happening to the Rohingyas.

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:31.720
But had it happened to the
Cham Muslims in the 1970s.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:33.880
And Ne Win had visited Cambodia

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.890
and gone back to Burma

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:38.480
to implement something that had turned out

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:40.110
to be very similar.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:41.600
So there's a connection

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:44.700
that I don't think has been detected,

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:48.330
or certainly not enough made
of it between these two cases

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:51.033
that we cover in this book.

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:56.960
Thailand was never colonized,

00:28:56.960 --> 00:29:01.230
by the Western powers in Southeast Asia.

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:03.910
It was the only country like Ethiopia.

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:06.810
In Africa it was the only
country in Southeast Asia

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:08.330
that was not colonized.

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:11.640
However, it didn't escape
the post-war violence

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:13.670
as Eve has pointed out.

00:29:13.670 --> 00:29:15.690
That the other countries of Southeast Asia

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:16.933
were subjected to.

00:29:17.870 --> 00:29:20.660
Indeed Thailand was drawn deeply

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:25.660
into the massive waves
of post-war violence

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:28.163
that affected Southeast Asia.

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:35.160
Three quarters of the bombing
raids over north Vietnam

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:41.040
were by planes that took off
from air bases in Thailand.

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:45.540
And this had something to do
with violence within Thailand,

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:49.730
as well as the former
foreign minister of Thailand.

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:54.730
Thanat Khoman later complained
the civilians like himself,

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:57.160
a foreign minister.

00:29:57.160 --> 00:29:59.250
He said didn't even know

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:02.670
that the Thai military were building

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:05.120
these new bases in Thailand.

00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:06.910
Was only later they found out

00:30:06.910 --> 00:30:08.880
that the United States bases

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:10.900
that were used for the
bombing of North Vietnam

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:11.830
were being built.

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:14.480
The civilians, although in the government

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:16.713
in the cabinet were not informed.

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:21.500
Thailand also said 25
battalions of special forces

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:26.500
to participate in the secret war in Laos.

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:29.040
So Thailand was drawn in

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:34.040
to these international
campaigns of violence

00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:38.230
that it had managed to escape

00:30:38.230 --> 00:30:40.673
in the colonial period before 1945.

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:45.780
Another example of the connections

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:49.600
is a country that managed
to keep out the war

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:53.090
under the reign of Prince
Sihanouk in Cambodia

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:56.510
was Prince Sihanouk, he
managed to do that partly

00:30:57.780 --> 00:31:01.110
by his Alliance with China.

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.490
He maintained a neutral
foreign policy for Cambodia

00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:06.090
during the 1960s.

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:10.683
And he saw China as a
mentor or a figure of power

00:31:13.520 --> 00:31:17.370
that guaranteed his
neutrality in the Cold War.

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:20.520
He visited China in October of 1965

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:24.597
for its National Day October 1st.

00:31:26.035 --> 00:31:30.830
What he didn't know was when
he went to pay homage to China,

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:34.480
Pol Pot was there on the same day,

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:35.960
secretly visiting China.

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:40.930
Pol Pot the secret head of the
communist party of Kampuchea,

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:44.160
the underground opposition
to Prince Sihanouk.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:46.160
The Chinese and Pol Pot himself

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:49.000
kept the secret from Prince Sihanouk.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:51.320
The head of state of Cambodia.

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:53.020
The two Cambodians were there,

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:54.330
but only Pol Pot knew

00:31:54.330 --> 00:31:56.930
that the other party Sihanouk was there.

00:31:56.930 --> 00:32:01.530
Now, what else happened
on October 1st, 1965,

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:03.720
the coup in Indonesia.

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:07.910
Which was also covered by Jeffrey
Robinson's opening chapter

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:08.743
in our book.

00:32:09.860 --> 00:32:13.720
What was important for
Cambodia was the mass slaughter

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:17.910
of the Indonesian communist
party that began on that day.

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:22.550
October 1st China's National
Day by accident, I'm sure.

00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:27.550
But the importance of it
was, Pol Pot later said it

00:32:28.851 --> 00:32:32.330
in 1977 in a retrospective history

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.807
of the communist movement in (indistinct).

00:32:34.807 --> 00:32:39.060
If we hadn't been in that
position, the same position

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:40.750
of the communist in Indonesia.

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:45.750
That is an unarmed, relatively
peaceful communist movement,

00:32:48.060 --> 00:32:50.170
we he would have been in greater danger

00:32:50.170 --> 00:32:51.670
than the communists in Indonesia.

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:55.190
But we weren't, we decided
that we wouldn't do that.

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:58.630
We would stage a rebellion
against Sihanouk's regime,

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:00.630
which they later did.

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:04.890
They were looking at the
communists a disastrous experience

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:09.773
in Indonesia on October
1st and afterwards in 1965.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:12.330
And so the Pol Pot decided

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:16.070
that he wasn't gonna put himself in danger

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:18.560
of what happened to the
communists in Indonesia.

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:21.560
Instead he was gonna do that exactly,

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:23.720
to his opponents in Cambodia,

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:27.743
which he did when he
came to power in 1975.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:30.910
Another example of the connections

00:33:30.910 --> 00:33:32.710
between the Southeast Asian countries

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:35.620
that we cover in this book

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:38.420
that they contributors cover very well,

00:33:38.420 --> 00:33:43.420
I think is the connections
between countries

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.360
that are not usually linked.

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:51.130
Malaya, The Philippines and South Vietnam.

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:55.440
Now these connections existed,
political connections,

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:59.313
but they also show how
different these countries are.

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:06.430
The Philippines became
independent in 1946.

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:11.662
It was immediately struck by

00:34:11.662 --> 00:34:15.270
an agrarian communist
revolution or a revolt

00:34:15.270 --> 00:34:19.930
by the Huk agrarian rebels.

00:34:19.930 --> 00:34:23.640
And it took place in Central Luzon.

00:34:23.640 --> 00:34:26.820
And one of the people who took part

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:30.800
in the successful defeat of that rebellion

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:36.500
as an advisor to president
Magsaysay was Edward Lansdale.

00:34:36.500 --> 00:34:38.023
Colonel Edward Lansdale.

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:43.840
Who helped design with
Magsaysay's reforms,

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:48.840
some agrarian reforms that
managed to undercut the appeal

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:51.900
of the Huk Rebels in Central Luzon

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:53.963
and bring that rebellion to an end.

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:58.840
Later when the insurgency

00:34:58.840 --> 00:35:01.120
in South Vietnam gathered force

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:04.780
Lansdale was transferred to south Vietnam.

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:09.013
And took a position as an advisor
to President Ngo Dinh Diem

00:35:09.013 --> 00:35:11.090
of South Vietnam.

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:12.700
And became an important...

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:14.670
I think he opened the second...

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:19.670
There were two CIA stations
in South Vietnam in 1954.

00:35:22.360 --> 00:35:27.360
Meanwhile, in Malaya
the British had held on,

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:30.730
unlike in the Americans
in the Philippines,

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:35.730
held on to their colony in
Malaya, as late as 1957.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:38.920
They have been similar,
to a Malayan coming

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:41.423
to start a rebellion from 1948.

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:44.707
And the British had fought that rebellion

00:35:44.707 --> 00:35:46.670
in what was called The Emergency,

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:48.690
the Malayan Emergency.

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:51.900
And one of the British
advisors, military advisers

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:56.630
who helped the British
put down The Emergency,

00:35:56.630 --> 00:35:59.980
the Malayan communist party revolt was...

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:02.090
What was this name?

00:36:05.395 --> 00:36:07.940
I've forgotten his name right now?

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:12.940
But he was a key figure

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:18.150
in the British successful
cabin insurgency.

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:20.000
He was later transferred to-

00:36:20.000 --> 00:36:21.100
(indistinct chatter)

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:22.104
Sorry.

00:36:22.104 --> 00:36:23.981
- I think the last name was Thompson.

00:36:23.981 --> 00:36:24.920
He was part of an-

00:36:24.920 --> 00:36:27.760
- Thompson, Sir. Robert
Thompson, thanks Michelle.

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:30.260
Sir. Robert Thompson was later transferred

00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:35.260
to South Vietnam at the advice of

00:36:35.699 --> 00:36:38.550
Tunku Abdul Rahman the
Prime Minister of Malaya

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:41.230
after independence in 1957.

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:46.230
And he was then made such an
impression on Ngo Dinh Diem,

00:36:46.980 --> 00:36:49.490
that Diem asked the British government

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:52.190
to send him back to South Vietnam

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:55.940
as head of the British
Military Advisory Mission.

00:36:55.940 --> 00:37:00.940
And he went back to South
Vietnam and according to the CIA.

00:37:04.357 --> 00:37:06.180
CIA head, George Carver,

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:09.610
Sir. Robert Thompson and
Ngo Dinh Diem together

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:14.610
devised the Strategic Hamlet
Program in South Vietnam.

00:37:16.520 --> 00:37:19.760
Now they've been very
successful, the British in Malaya

00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:22.840
in undercutting the insurgency there.

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:25.520
And the Americans and Magsaysay
have been very successful

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:28.330
in undercutting the
insurgency in the Philippines

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:31.790
with those two experiences
were successful.

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:34.560
But in South Vietnam, they weren't.

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:37.050
The Strategic Hamlets where a failure,

00:37:37.050 --> 00:37:42.050
the NLF or Viet Cong insurgency
grew larger and larger.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:46.190
And eventually was nearly
successful in 1965.

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:49.670
At the time the Americans intervened

00:37:49.670 --> 00:37:52.460
with ground troops in 1965.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:56.650
Why was that not the case like it was

00:37:56.650 --> 00:37:58.910
in the Philippines and Malaya?

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:01.780
Because South Vietnam was
a very different society

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:03.750
than those two countries.

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:06.750
The insurgency was quite circumscribed

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:09.490
in both Malaya and the Philippines.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:13.890
Regionalized or ethnically
circumscribed in Malaya.

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:18.623
Whereas in South Vietnam
land inequalities were great,

00:38:20.130 --> 00:38:21.530
not only in the Mekong Delta.

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:25.900
But also along the Southern
and central Vietnamese coast.

00:38:25.900 --> 00:38:29.200
And the Strategic Hamlet Program

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:33.626
had to be much larger and more unpopular

00:38:33.626 --> 00:38:35.720
and eventually failed.

00:38:35.720 --> 00:38:38.720
So South Vietnam was
quite a different society

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:40.210
than the Philippines and Malaya.

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:43.360
So although the connections existed

00:38:43.360 --> 00:38:46.070
between those countries and the strategies

00:38:46.070 --> 00:38:47.759
that were adopted were quite similar,

00:38:47.759 --> 00:38:49.520
they failed in South Vietnam

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:53.603
because of the different societies there.

00:38:56.097 --> 00:38:59.710
There is also a difference
in the countries

00:38:59.710 --> 00:39:04.560
that experienced violence
after World War II,

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:05.853
and those that didn't.

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:09.220
Burma and Indonesia, for instance,

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:11.970
particularly in the 1950s,

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:16.970
experienced democratic
flourishing, in the 1950s.

00:39:17.750 --> 00:39:22.750
So under Sukarno in
Indonesia and U Nu in Burma.

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:26.110
Despite the challenge of a Korean revolt

00:39:26.110 --> 00:39:28.530
and a communist revolt in Burma.

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:30.363
Both starting in 1948,

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:35.270
Burma was a successful
democracy until the overthrow

00:39:35.270 --> 00:39:38.230
by the military under Ne Win in 1962.

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:42.290
Similarly Indonesia was
a multi-party democracy

00:39:42.290 --> 00:39:45.380
until around 1958, when Sukarno

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:48.290
instituted what we call
a guided democracy,

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:53.290
which shaded into the
military takeover of 1965.

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:58.580
But those two examples in the
1950s show that Southeast Asia

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:01.110
its largest country on the mainland,

00:40:01.110 --> 00:40:03.580
at least geographically was Burma.

00:40:03.580 --> 00:40:06.317
And in Indonesia, the largest
country in Southeast Asia

00:40:06.317 --> 00:40:08.524
didn't have the experience

00:40:08.524 --> 00:40:12.370
of at least temporarily
successful democracy.

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:16.520
And that is now the case once again,

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:21.520
after a long Suharto era, ending in 1998.

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:26.863
So we do have some examples
of heterogeneity, diversity

00:40:26.863 --> 00:40:30.040
and of the variety of possibilities

00:40:30.040 --> 00:40:35.040
that are in the history of
post-war Southeast Asia.

00:40:37.070 --> 00:40:39.910
Finally, I'd like to mention the chapter

00:40:39.910 --> 00:40:43.610
by Hoang Min Vu on Cambodia.

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:48.160
Which is a nice way to
sum up what I'm saying,

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:52.410
because it's a hard hitting
attack on all of the parties

00:40:52.410 --> 00:40:56.820
to the Cambodian disaster.

00:40:56.820 --> 00:41:01.640
All of the international global responses

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:04.520
to the genocide in Cambodia

00:41:04.520 --> 00:41:07.000
come in for a fierce criticism

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:09.020
on the part of Hoang Minh Vu.

00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:09.853
It's worth reading,

00:41:09.853 --> 00:41:14.820
I think because he is unstinting
in his critique of Vietnam,

00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:20.140
of Asyan, of the United States, of China.

00:41:20.140 --> 00:41:23.730
And he ends with a kicker.

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:25.080
I'm not gonna tell you what it is,

00:41:25.080 --> 00:41:29.833
but I suggest that you read
to the end of his chapter.

00:41:29.833 --> 00:41:33.000
But I'll leave the rest to Eve to finish.

00:41:33.000 --> 00:41:33.833
Thank you.

00:41:33.833 --> 00:41:34.840
- Thank you, Ben.

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:38.129
Really interesting as always.

00:41:38.129 --> 00:41:42.744
So Ben talked about some of
the historical connections

00:41:42.744 --> 00:41:45.650
and I'm gonna go through
probably fairly quickly

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:50.650
some of the conceptual
thematic connections.

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:53.544
So how long do we actually have here?

00:41:53.544 --> 00:41:55.820
- [Erik] We could end by 01:00

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:57.890
so we can make sure there's time for Q&amp;A.

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:00.510
- Okay, so I will probably
go in some more in depth

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:02.260
and then some will go more quickly.

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:08.180
So there are two sets of conceptual themes

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:09.620
that sort of show up immediately

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:10.850
at the beginning of the book.

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:12.280
And one of them is the...

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:14.579
Well, they're both actually
in the introduction,

00:42:14.579 --> 00:42:19.020
but somebody who's looking
at the book might notice

00:42:19.020 --> 00:42:22.500
that the way things are organized
in the table of contents

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:24.100
are a bit different than they're discussed

00:42:24.100 --> 00:42:26.170
earlier on in the chapters.

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:28.363
And there's a reason for that.

00:42:29.420 --> 00:42:32.280
These chapters could be
organized in a multitude of ways

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:34.910
and can be thought of
in a multitude of ways.

00:42:34.910 --> 00:42:37.370
And that's really part
of the goal of the book.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:40.780
Is that people can look
at the different chapters,

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:44.410
the different cases and understand
connections between them.

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:46.792
Historical connections,
regional connections,

00:42:46.792 --> 00:42:48.910
and other thematic connections.

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:53.910
And hopefully it's
useful then for students

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:55.720
and also for people teaching

00:42:55.720 --> 00:42:57.983
about Southeast Asia or mass violence.

00:42:59.230 --> 00:43:02.530
So we wanted to just draw
out some of those themes

00:43:02.530 --> 00:43:06.760
that may not altogether be obvious.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:09.085
Some are obvious, some
are a little bit less.

00:43:09.085 --> 00:43:14.085
So here are some initial
themes to consider

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:17.920
and I'll discuss at least a couple of them

00:43:17.920 --> 00:43:20.550
in a little more depth.

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:23.480
So there are social and
political transformation

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:25.400
during periods of change

00:43:25.400 --> 00:43:28.480
and during periods of transformation,

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:29.973
as I'll discuss in a moment.

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:32.243
It's a very vulnerable time,

00:43:32.243 --> 00:43:34.453
a potentially very dangerous time.

00:43:35.860 --> 00:43:38.010
There's this ambiguity.

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:39.600
Ambiguity of identities,

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:42.200
ambiguities of intent.

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:44.320
Also liminality, marginality,

00:43:44.320 --> 00:43:46.070
people who live in marginal places,

00:43:46.070 --> 00:43:49.180
who occupy marginal places within society,

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:52.800
or literally on the
border or whose identities

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:55.820
for some reason are seen as uncertain.

00:43:55.820 --> 00:43:58.830
There's the political imagination.

00:43:58.830 --> 00:44:00.840
Which is not something
that's always considered,

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:04.030
but how people imagine the other,

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:05.570
how people imagine nations,

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:08.453
how people imagine who other people are.

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.840
This all comes into play
during these periods.

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:14.410
I mentioned earlier,

00:44:14.410 --> 00:44:18.070
the impunity of leaders
and the role of leaders

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:21.373
and then talked quite a
bit about that as well.

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:27.550
And there's also the haunting
of the past and the present.

00:44:27.680 --> 00:44:30.560
Which tends to be the enduring...

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:34.570
The enduring not only consequences,

00:44:34.570 --> 00:44:37.560
but shadow of genocides and mass violence

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:40.920
that continues to haunt
societies in various ways.

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:45.920
And then just briefly which
we'll talk about in a moment,

00:44:46.570 --> 00:44:50.590
killing as both as a technical
procedure on the one hand,

00:44:50.590 --> 00:44:52.830
which is very different
than another type of killing

00:44:52.830 --> 00:44:55.450
that takes place, that's
much more visceral.

00:44:55.450 --> 00:44:58.330
And you see that in different cases.

00:44:58.330 --> 00:45:00.580
It's a really interesting question

00:45:00.580 --> 00:45:04.020
of why that is so and
how are they the same

00:45:04.020 --> 00:45:05.250
and how are they different?

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:06.083
And that's it really,

00:45:06.083 --> 00:45:08.210
I think actually an area to be explored

00:45:08.210 --> 00:45:11.160
in far more depth than it has been.

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:16.150
So social and political transformation,

00:45:16.150 --> 00:45:19.770
as it's been mentioned.

00:45:19.770 --> 00:45:24.770
He talked about how in some
Southeast Asian societies

00:45:24.850 --> 00:45:27.470
like in Indonesia,

00:45:27.470 --> 00:45:32.470
they went from democracy to
then when Suharto comes in

00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:37.990
and massacres hundreds
of thousands of people.

00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:40.793
A million people or so.

00:45:42.654 --> 00:45:44.140
That's been happening

00:45:44.140 --> 00:45:48.390
within this transition from
democracy into a dictatorship.

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:50.450
And then you have other situations

00:45:50.450 --> 00:45:54.860
whereas you have in Burma
where they move from democracy.

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:56.500
During that transition,

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:59.820
you have the violence
perpetrated against the Rohingya.

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:02.640
Now it's interesting in Southeast Asia

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:03.473
in thinking about...

00:46:03.473 --> 00:46:06.943
Coming from my anthropological
point of view here.

00:46:07.890 --> 00:46:11.860
When you think about Southeast
Asia about transitions,

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:13.750
about transformations.

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:17.020
In life cycle rituals or in life cycle...

00:46:17.910 --> 00:46:21.240
During pregnancy, during
birth, during death,

00:46:21.240 --> 00:46:23.990
it's considered a very fruitful

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:26.530
and yet very delicate process.

00:46:26.530 --> 00:46:28.910
Great care must be taken
because there's a danger

00:46:28.910 --> 00:46:31.560
in this process, when something is moving

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:33.240
from one state to another,

00:46:33.240 --> 00:46:35.460
when something's coming into being.

00:46:35.460 --> 00:46:37.360
And I think it's a useful way

00:46:37.360 --> 00:46:39.520
of thinking about these periods also.

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:42.650
Of transitions of moving
towards independence,

00:46:42.650 --> 00:46:47.493
or changing of political
systems and so forth.

00:46:49.489 --> 00:46:50.740
There's an uncertainty there,

00:46:50.740 --> 00:46:53.260
there's a vulnerability
there, there's a danger there.

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:56.810
And in this sort of context,
you end up with situations

00:46:56.810 --> 00:47:00.100
where people feel insecure.

00:47:00.100 --> 00:47:02.090
And then they often...

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:05.998
In some cases, if we saw
in Burma and so forth,

00:47:05.998 --> 00:47:10.500
they end up finding people that...

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:13.623
Identifying people as imposters.

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:19.000
Sorry, Azeem Ibrahim
talks about this notion

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.770
of the imposter and the outsider,

00:47:21.770 --> 00:47:26.120
but so does Elliott
Prasse-Freeman and Andrew Ong,

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:30.250
who talked about exclusion and
inclusion within the state.

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:33.750
And this theme you see
comes up quite a lot.

00:47:33.750 --> 00:47:35.440
Certainly through all
the Myanmar readings,

00:47:35.440 --> 00:47:38.310
but also certainly in Cambodia.

00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:41.360
Where you had Kosal Path and Tram Luong

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:44.710
talking about the ethnic Vietnamese

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:46.190
during the Lon Nol period

00:47:46.190 --> 00:47:51.190
who were seen as in some cases,
as demons and as the enemy

00:47:52.730 --> 00:47:56.040
and as essentially
masking their true intent,

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:58.010
which the intention connects up

00:47:58.010 --> 00:48:00.980
with Katherine Munyan's chapter.

00:48:00.980 --> 00:48:03.650
So I'm looking, and I'm seeing
I have three minutes left.

00:48:03.650 --> 00:48:07.330
So I'll just quickly
go through the slides.

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:11.330
So at least you can see where
some of the chapters sit

00:48:11.330 --> 00:48:14.740
with some of these different themes.

00:48:14.740 --> 00:48:17.120
So with the ambiguity and liminality,

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:18.020
I discuss it a bit.

00:48:18.020 --> 00:48:21.150
It's obviously very connected to the theme

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:24.253
of transformation and transition.

00:48:26.290 --> 00:48:29.350
But one of the things that
happens in these contexts

00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:32.380
and (indistinct) talks about this as well

00:48:32.380 --> 00:48:34.690
in terms of ethnic violence.

00:48:34.690 --> 00:48:38.540
That there's this language
that starts occurring

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:40.160
at the language of the imposter

00:48:40.160 --> 00:48:42.300
in these contexts of violence.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:45.770
And this counterfeit person somehow

00:48:45.770 --> 00:48:48.040
and counterfeit identities.

00:48:48.040 --> 00:48:53.030
And that plays out and you see
this an Al McCoy's chapter,

00:48:53.030 --> 00:48:54.890
where he talks about Duterte.

00:48:54.890 --> 00:48:57.350
You have this situation where

00:48:57.350 --> 00:49:00.880
the people being targeted
are the marginal community

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:04.850
of what are the so-called
drug users and dealers

00:49:04.850 --> 00:49:08.890
who are being picked
out as, you know not...

00:49:08.890 --> 00:49:09.920
In this case,

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:14.490
excluding as Elliott Prasse-Freeman

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:15.850
and Andrew Ong talk about.

00:49:15.850 --> 00:49:17.950
They're some of the people
that are pushed out,

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:20.483
excluded from society then.

00:49:23.640 --> 00:49:28.640
You also have the topics of
the theme of impunity, silence,

00:49:28.750 --> 00:49:30.530
and rule of the leaders.

00:49:30.530 --> 00:49:34.810
While silence and impunity
are obviously linked,

00:49:34.810 --> 00:49:37.313
they also can be treated separately.

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:42.200
Well, silence certainly
contributes to impunity.

00:49:42.200 --> 00:49:46.170
It also rests on its own impunity

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:47.883
as other sources as well.

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:51.190
Several of the (indistinct)
chapters talk about impunity.

00:49:51.190 --> 00:49:54.890
Particularly though in
Tyrell Haberkon's chapter

00:49:55.760 --> 00:50:00.760
on Thailand, and also in
Pawakapan's chapter also Thailand,

00:50:01.430 --> 00:50:03.300
and also in Laura McGrew's chapter

00:50:03.300 --> 00:50:05.060
who talks about the...

00:50:05.948 --> 00:50:09.172
Who talks about Cambodia and the post war

00:50:09.172 --> 00:50:11.363
in the 90s, 2000s era.

00:50:12.310 --> 00:50:16.420
And the amount of impunity
that government has had there

00:50:16.420 --> 00:50:18.980
with violence that's been perpetrated

00:50:18.980 --> 00:50:21.573
since the Khmer Rouge by the state.

00:50:26.027 --> 00:50:28.490
Just quickly I'll just share this one

00:50:28.490 --> 00:50:32.038
technical versus visceral violence.

00:50:32.038 --> 00:50:35.810
So by technical violence,

00:50:35.810 --> 00:50:37.180
what's meant here is violence

00:50:37.180 --> 00:50:40.810
that's very much removed from the person.

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:42.990
For example, the aerial bombings

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:45.990
that Sophie Quinn Judge
talks about in her chapter,

00:50:45.990 --> 00:50:50.450
in Vietnam, by the US or the
aerial bombings in Vietnam,

00:50:52.800 --> 00:50:54.430
during the first Indochinese war.

00:50:54.430 --> 00:50:57.400
Talked about in Christian Lentz's chapter

00:50:57.400 --> 00:50:59.380
In Daniel Bultmann's chapter,

00:50:59.380 --> 00:51:02.680
which takes place in the prison

00:51:02.680 --> 00:51:05.680
and torture center of Tuol Sleng.

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:07.630
It's a very kind of different type

00:51:08.538 --> 00:51:10.690
of technical violence there.

00:51:10.690 --> 00:51:12.790
The body is very much involved,

00:51:12.790 --> 00:51:15.710
but not involved in the
kind of intimate way

00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:17.340
with the visceral violence.

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:21.440
Instead, the bodies are
treated as mere resources.

00:51:21.440 --> 00:51:25.080
So they're harvested for blood.

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:26.740
They're harvested for bile.

00:51:26.740 --> 00:51:29.720
The gallbladders are
removed, all to sort of serve

00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:34.720
the purposes of the revolution
of the Khmer Rouge purpose.

00:51:37.630 --> 00:51:38.970
And so there you have...

00:51:38.970 --> 00:51:39.930
It's very clinical.

00:51:39.930 --> 00:51:42.830
So there's these medical
experiments on the one hand,

00:51:42.830 --> 00:51:46.010
but on the other hand, it's
kind of like factory farming

00:51:46.010 --> 00:51:47.340
in a sense on the other.

00:51:47.340 --> 00:51:51.373
That the bodies are just simply processed.

00:51:52.520 --> 00:51:54.923
Visceral several chapters cover that.

00:51:57.770 --> 00:51:59.440
And let's see.

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:00.853
Since we're out of time.

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:05.080
There is the political imagination,

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:08.000
which I mentioned little bit earlier.

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:11.580
And this has to do with
cruising several chapters

00:52:11.580 --> 00:52:15.620
of how people are imagining
themselves as citizens

00:52:15.620 --> 00:52:19.130
or how they imagine
citizenship altogether.

00:52:19.130 --> 00:52:22.900
How the nation is imagined (indistinct)

00:52:24.308 --> 00:52:27.763
comes in here and also
in terms of the other.

00:52:33.180 --> 00:52:34.760
The Haunting of the Present in the Past,

00:52:34.760 --> 00:52:36.799
I'll just say Tram Long's chapter

00:52:36.799 --> 00:52:40.010
really goes into this into some depth.

00:52:40.010 --> 00:52:42.100
A really fascinating chapter,

00:52:42.100 --> 00:52:44.960
which talks about the
massacre of the Vietnamese

00:52:44.960 --> 00:52:49.501
in the early 70s under Lon Nol.

00:52:49.501 --> 00:52:54.501
And these killings were
propagated by the government.

00:52:54.890 --> 00:52:59.513
And she chose the juxtaposition
between on the one hand,

00:53:00.610 --> 00:53:05.610
this (indistinct) hotel on the
river ways in (indistinct).

00:53:06.120 --> 00:53:09.900
Reflecting on the shimmering
surface of the water there.

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:14.340
The (indistinct) hotel is
owned by a Vietnamese tycoon

00:53:14.340 --> 00:53:17.660
and that's contrasted
with underneath the waters

00:53:17.660 --> 00:53:20.330
is still the bodies of
the hundreds of thousands

00:53:20.330 --> 00:53:23.440
of Vietnamese victims that
were killed in the 1970s

00:53:23.440 --> 00:53:26.803
that continue to haunt today.

00:53:28.010 --> 00:53:31.060
And now with continued ethnic tensions

00:53:33.050 --> 00:53:36.820
and racism against the
Vietnamese living in Cambodia,

00:53:36.820 --> 00:53:40.670
we can see how that
haunting sits beside that.

00:53:44.150 --> 00:53:45.780
So we are out of time.

00:53:45.780 --> 00:53:49.203
So that is the organization
of the book and the chapters.

