00:55:57.740 --> 00:55:58.807
- [Audience member] Thank you.

00:55:58.807 --> 00:56:03.500
And thank you both for the
book and for the presentation.

00:56:03.500 --> 00:56:05.070
My question...

00:56:05.070 --> 00:56:05.903
Excuse me.

00:56:05.903 --> 00:56:07.980
Is something that comes
out of me listening

00:56:07.980 --> 00:56:11.060
to the parallels that you both spoke of.

00:56:11.060 --> 00:56:14.770
It's the clear and obvious
role that communist parties,

00:56:14.770 --> 00:56:18.900
whether as insurgencies or as
political oppositions played.

00:56:18.900 --> 00:56:20.780
And I wonder if you can speak to,

00:56:20.780 --> 00:56:25.780
is there a comment or parallel
origin of these movements?

00:56:26.710 --> 00:56:31.710
Were they trained in China as
the Chinese Communist Party

00:56:33.160 --> 00:56:34.972
during the Chinese revolution?

00:56:34.972 --> 00:56:36.930
Were they similarly inspired?

00:56:36.930 --> 00:56:40.780
was it a training in Europe or was it

00:56:40.780 --> 00:56:45.220
each country has a sort of different root.

00:56:45.220 --> 00:56:48.683
A different root of communist
opposition and insurgency.

00:56:54.610 --> 00:56:56.713
- I'm gonna let you have this one.

00:56:57.720 --> 00:56:59.733
- I think that there is a variety

00:57:02.780 --> 00:57:05.880
of different kinds of
political inspiration

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:09.550
for the many different and diverse

00:57:09.550 --> 00:57:14.550
kinds of political and mass
violence that we cover.

00:57:14.610 --> 00:57:19.335
I think some of the most extreme cases

00:57:19.335 --> 00:57:22.450
range from the Communist
Party of Kampuchea

00:57:22.450 --> 00:57:23.823
and the Pol Pot regime,

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:29.660
to the military dictatorship
in Indonesia, under Suharto,

00:57:29.660 --> 00:57:34.030
which was an anticommunist
purge of communists.

00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:37.970
Mass murder of communists.

00:57:37.970 --> 00:57:39.870
There are cases in between

00:57:40.920 --> 00:57:43.660
which perhaps are exemplified

00:57:43.660 --> 00:57:46.330
by the Burmese military dictatorship.

00:57:46.330 --> 00:57:51.330
Which was inspired by
socialism in one country.

00:57:51.610 --> 00:57:54.950
I think they call themselves
the Burmese road to socialism.

00:57:54.950 --> 00:57:57.340
But it was definitely
a military dictatorship

00:57:57.340 --> 00:58:00.300
and was opposed by the different branches

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:02.860
of the communist party in Burma.

00:58:02.860 --> 00:58:05.680
And definitely repressed the...

00:58:08.420 --> 00:58:11.100
There are a number of different factions

00:58:11.100 --> 00:58:14.520
of Southeast Asian communism.

00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:18.200
That took a number of different roads.

00:58:18.200 --> 00:58:22.900
That the Malayan Communist
Party was very pro China.

00:58:22.900 --> 00:58:24.420
The Vietnamese Communist Party

00:58:24.420 --> 00:58:26.780
was more independent of China.

00:58:26.780 --> 00:58:31.780
There is a common
chronological theme in 1948,

00:58:32.840 --> 00:58:36.810
when many of the communist
parties rose up in rebellion

00:58:36.810 --> 00:58:41.810
from Indonesia to Burma to Malaya.

00:58:43.060 --> 00:58:47.340
And that was often
thought to have come from

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:49.230
a meeting in Calcutta in 1948.

00:58:52.043 --> 00:58:56.452
And it was alleged that
Soviet instructions

00:58:56.452 --> 00:58:59.330
or (indistinct) instructions
led to many of the

00:58:59.330 --> 00:59:00.800
Southeast Asian communist parties

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:03.360
staging rebellions at the same time.

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:06.723
But there's no consensus on that.

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:12.630
Regional complicity or
regional coordination

00:59:12.630 --> 00:59:14.960
amongst historians about that.

00:59:14.960 --> 00:59:19.960
I've seen historians debunk that theory.

00:59:20.070 --> 00:59:25.070
Although the historical or
chronological concatenation

00:59:27.450 --> 00:59:29.013
seems pretty convincing.

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:33.348
But I think what strikes me more

00:59:33.348 --> 00:59:38.348
is the across the board violence

00:59:39.522 --> 00:59:43.350
that is characteristic of the
Cold War in Southeast Asia.

00:59:43.350 --> 00:59:44.600
Other people might disagree,

00:59:44.600 --> 00:59:47.160
but it seems to me that there is...

00:59:47.160 --> 00:59:48.350
Looking at the violence

00:59:48.350 --> 00:59:53.082
in places like Thailand, Burma, Vietnam,

00:59:53.082 --> 00:59:58.082
both North and South Indonesia,
the Philippines, Malaysia,

01:00:02.981 --> 01:00:06.020
and also the appearance
in all of those places.

01:00:06.020 --> 01:00:09.570
Nearly all of them of
democratic potential.

01:00:09.570 --> 01:00:14.500
One case that struck me,
just reading this morning,

01:00:14.500 --> 01:00:18.173
was in Brunei, which we
don't cover in this book.

01:00:19.049 --> 01:00:24.049
The elections that were
held in 1962 for some

01:00:27.300 --> 01:00:30.770
only partially democratic participation

01:00:30.770 --> 01:00:35.060
of the British were
still the colonial power.

01:00:35.060 --> 01:00:37.660
There was the Monarch,
the Sultan of Brunei,

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:42.080
but elections were held for
a representative government

01:00:42.080 --> 01:00:43.840
on a partial level.

01:00:43.840 --> 01:00:48.840
And the People's Party
Brunei won all 16 seats.

01:00:50.487 --> 01:00:55.487
It was a landslide by
representing the popular vote.

01:00:56.930 --> 01:00:59.270
And they weren't gonna
get what they wanted.

01:00:59.270 --> 01:01:03.680
They didn't get either
full political power

01:01:03.680 --> 01:01:08.003
or what they wanted was not
to be included in Malaysia.

01:01:09.471 --> 01:01:14.471
And so they chose the
most disastrous option,

01:01:16.240 --> 01:01:18.260
which was to revolt.

01:01:18.260 --> 01:01:21.530
And they staged a violent revolt

01:01:21.530 --> 01:01:23.840
and were crushed by the British.

01:01:23.840 --> 01:01:28.840
And Burma is still a
monarchical dictatorship.

01:01:29.430 --> 01:01:32.853
So where do we go with that information?

01:01:34.462 --> 01:01:38.623
What are the illogical
inspiration to attribute that to?

01:01:39.500 --> 01:01:43.700
It seems that there is a
wide range of possibilities

01:01:43.700 --> 01:01:44.783
in Southeast Asia.

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:53.000
- We have a question in the chat.

01:01:53.000 --> 01:01:57.060
So I'll take that first
in the spirit of inclusion

01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:57.970
and I'm gonna read it

01:01:57.970 --> 01:01:59.550
because it's difficult for others to see

01:01:59.550 --> 01:02:00.850
from the back of the room.

01:02:02.280 --> 01:02:05.010
The story Ben Kiernan told about linkages

01:02:05.010 --> 01:02:07.740
between violence in Cambodia and Burma,

01:02:07.740 --> 01:02:09.890
including the possibility of meetings

01:02:09.890 --> 01:02:13.530
between Pol Pot and Ne Win
was absolutely fascinating.

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:16.460
I wonder if you could
explain on the dilemma...

01:02:16.460 --> 01:02:17.700
Expand on the dilemma,

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:20.930
these types of cases
present for historian.

01:02:20.930 --> 01:02:23.700
On the one hand, the
circumstantial evidence

01:02:23.700 --> 01:02:26.700
is too great to ignore, monkey's uncle.

01:02:26.700 --> 01:02:28.680
On the other hand, historians

01:02:28.680 --> 01:02:31.010
tend not to prize circumstantial evidence

01:02:31.010 --> 01:02:33.520
and availability of archival records

01:02:33.520 --> 01:02:36.700
is challenging and patchy
at the best at times.

01:02:36.700 --> 01:02:39.940
How then should historian
seek to uncover and understand

01:02:39.940 --> 01:02:44.600
the hidden circuitry that
linked cases of mass violence.

01:02:44.600 --> 01:02:46.653
And that's from Mathias Fibinger.

01:02:49.800 --> 01:02:52.640
- Yes, it's quite true that I can't prove

01:02:52.640 --> 01:02:54.840
that Pol Pot met with Ne Win

01:02:56.411 --> 01:02:59.550
Maybe one day we can check this.

01:02:59.550 --> 01:03:03.320
I don't think it's gonna be documented.

01:03:03.320 --> 01:03:05.800
It would possibly be documented by now

01:03:05.800 --> 01:03:07.560
in the Cambodian records.

01:03:07.560 --> 01:03:11.160
It might be documentable
in the Burmese records.

01:03:11.160 --> 01:03:12.929
If it occurred.

01:03:12.929 --> 01:03:17.929
I just think that it's likely,
but I could be proven wrong.

01:03:20.260 --> 01:03:25.073
At any rate four meetings
between Nuon Chea and Ne Win

01:03:26.030 --> 01:03:29.550
with Nuon Chea having
been convicted of genocide

01:03:29.550 --> 01:03:31.720
against the Cham Muslims.

01:03:31.720 --> 01:03:36.280
And Ne Win starting off a
campaign a few months later

01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:39.890
of mass persecution of Burma's Muslims,

01:03:39.890 --> 01:03:44.810
seems to me to be likely
that they discussed

01:03:44.810 --> 01:03:48.720
their common interests in at least

01:03:48.720 --> 01:03:50.760
one of those four meetings.

01:03:50.760 --> 01:03:54.820
Whether that would leave you to conclude

01:03:54.820 --> 01:03:59.280
that there is a linkage
between the two cases

01:04:00.368 --> 01:04:02.350
both of which ended up in genocide

01:04:03.470 --> 01:04:07.013
in countries that were otherwise unlinked.

01:04:08.567 --> 01:04:12.740
Is of course, something
that ought to be explored.

01:04:12.740 --> 01:04:14.593
Which is what I'm saying.

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:19.060
That's the reference
to the monkey's uncle.

01:04:19.060 --> 01:04:23.100
But I think that Katherine
Munyan is quite right

01:04:23.100 --> 01:04:28.100
in her chapter, that there
were opportunities missed

01:04:28.850 --> 01:04:33.850
by service of the
Burmese case to point out

01:04:38.510 --> 01:04:43.510
the strong likelihood of
this Rohingya genocide

01:04:47.250 --> 01:04:48.323
adventure bagging.

01:04:49.360 --> 01:04:51.930
Not necessarily just on the basis

01:04:51.930 --> 01:04:54.290
of Ne Win's visit to Cambodia,

01:04:54.290 --> 01:04:58.350
but on the point she makes about intent

01:04:58.350 --> 01:05:03.350
and the evidence that existed over decades

01:05:05.920 --> 01:05:08.560
of persecution, of the Rohingya.

01:05:08.560 --> 01:05:12.510
That nonjudicial
organizations as she puts it,

01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:15.040
could have called for an investigation

01:05:15.040 --> 01:05:20.040
into whether a genocide
is ongoing or likely

01:05:21.760 --> 01:05:22.940
earlier than they did.

01:05:22.940 --> 01:05:24.690
And I think she's right about that.

01:05:30.885 --> 01:05:32.485
- (indistinct) your hand was up.

