01:05:51.780 --> 01:05:55.740
So, one thing that I really
appreciate is the connections

01:05:55.740 --> 01:05:58.690
precisely that you pointed out.

01:05:58.690 --> 01:06:01.210
Despite the diversity of the region.

01:06:01.210 --> 01:06:04.600
Now, what I'm interested in is
the opposite, in the center.

01:06:04.600 --> 01:06:07.563
Now give them this commonalities.

01:06:09.409 --> 01:06:11.150
If you haven't had the
chance to read the book,

01:06:11.150 --> 01:06:14.680
but clearly the many
episodes of mass violence

01:06:14.680 --> 01:06:16.723
that the book has covered,

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:23.273
there is a range in terms of
perhaps degree of violence,

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:26.543
causes of violence,

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:31.120
how violence perpetrated so forth.

01:06:31.120 --> 01:06:33.990
So an example, you gave is Khmer Rouge,

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:35.333
Killing in Khmer Rouge.

01:06:37.080 --> 01:06:39.780
The degree of violence
there is for instance

01:06:39.780 --> 01:06:42.360
certainly not the same as perhaps

01:06:42.360 --> 01:06:45.280
violence occurring under the Huk Rebellion

01:06:45.280 --> 01:06:46.200
in the Philippines.

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:47.790
So there's a range there.

01:06:47.790 --> 01:06:50.780
So I'm interested from your perspective,

01:06:50.780 --> 01:06:52.140
and your understanding,

01:06:52.140 --> 01:06:55.500
what might be the positive
factors that explain why

01:06:55.500 --> 01:06:59.760
in some cases there can
be perhaps more restrained

01:06:59.760 --> 01:07:04.560
or more factor to prevent
violence from escalating,

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:09.560
for instance to the extent
like Khmer Rouge and so forth.

01:07:10.013 --> 01:07:12.924
So I wanna explain variation

01:07:12.924 --> 01:07:14.878
in contrast and the commonalities.

01:07:14.878 --> 01:07:15.711
Thank you.

01:07:21.190 --> 01:07:23.220
- Thank you for your wonderful question.

01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:26.290
And it's really an excellent point.

01:07:26.290 --> 01:07:29.700
There is clearly a very large range

01:07:29.700 --> 01:07:32.970
in the various different
cases covered in the books

01:07:32.970 --> 01:07:35.910
and cases also beyond the book.

01:07:35.910 --> 01:07:40.340
In terms of degree and scale
and causes and so forth.

01:07:40.340 --> 01:07:43.420
And, that's one of the
things with taking on

01:07:43.420 --> 01:07:46.740
a big topic of political
violence in Southeast Asia

01:07:46.740 --> 01:07:49.160
and across really different regions

01:07:49.160 --> 01:07:52.410
and not even all exactly
in the same time period.

01:07:52.410 --> 01:07:54.623
That there are lots of different factors.

01:07:55.910 --> 01:07:58.040
Both in terms of global factors

01:07:58.040 --> 01:08:00.260
of the global politics that are going on.

01:08:00.260 --> 01:08:02.760
And also in terms of local politics

01:08:02.760 --> 01:08:07.360
and local culture as well.

01:08:07.360 --> 01:08:12.360
I think that apart from the
large thematic commonalities,

01:08:13.280 --> 01:08:16.720
for example, The Cold
War you had for example,

01:08:16.720 --> 01:08:20.050
aerial bombing in Cambodia.

01:08:20.050 --> 01:08:24.920
You had aerial bombing in
Laos and Vietnam as well.

01:08:27.050 --> 01:08:29.910
You still had within that same Cold War,

01:08:29.910 --> 01:08:31.040
you didn't have that same...

01:08:31.040 --> 01:08:34.170
Well, it played out in
other parts of the region,

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:36.640
for example, in Indonesia,

01:08:36.640 --> 01:08:38.900
there was no aerial bombing there.

01:08:38.900 --> 01:08:39.733
Why?

01:08:39.733 --> 01:08:42.882
Well, for one thing, there
was not an external source

01:08:42.882 --> 01:08:46.410
that's coming in with these
airplanes doing the bombing.

01:08:46.410 --> 01:08:49.680
Even though there was also bombing,

01:08:49.680 --> 01:08:52.090
within those countries as well.

01:08:52.090 --> 01:08:53.870
But the type of violence

01:08:53.870 --> 01:08:56.620
and the types of targets
that was happening

01:08:56.620 --> 01:08:58.980
was different in Indonesia.

01:08:58.980 --> 01:09:01.430
Mass killings in both cases.

01:09:01.430 --> 01:09:05.730
But they weren't trying to...

01:09:07.535 --> 01:09:10.100
Some of the aims of the regime itself

01:09:10.100 --> 01:09:15.100
and the ideologies of the
regimes were quite different.

01:09:15.900 --> 01:09:20.120
And there was of course, external players

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:21.820
in the Indonesian case as well.

01:09:21.820 --> 01:09:23.910
For example, you have the British,

01:09:23.910 --> 01:09:26.883
as it's been in all
the headlines recently.

01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:32.290
Through the release of
the various documents

01:09:32.290 --> 01:09:35.643
that had been released that
talk about their part in that.

01:09:36.482 --> 01:09:38.610
And there were certainly
the US role to play there.

01:09:38.610 --> 01:09:41.120
But it was secretive in
that case where it was over

01:09:41.120 --> 01:09:44.790
or in Vietnam.

01:09:44.790 --> 01:09:48.240
So I think that it's a very
large question that you ask

01:09:48.240 --> 01:09:50.620
and there's lots of differences,

01:09:50.620 --> 01:09:53.130
even in cases that may
happen at the same time,

01:09:53.130 --> 01:09:55.450
or even with some of the same players.

01:09:55.450 --> 01:09:58.500
But yeah, I think I'll stop there

01:10:00.518 --> 01:10:02.851
because it's a big question.

01:10:07.674 --> 01:10:09.420
- I'm gonna take one last
question for sake of time

01:10:09.420 --> 01:10:11.940
and I see that (indistinct)
has a question also.

01:10:11.940 --> 01:10:15.281
If you can introduce
yourself (indistinct).

01:10:15.281 --> 01:10:18.190
- Hi, thank you very much for your book.

01:10:19.537 --> 01:10:22.080
and your fascinating presentation.

01:10:22.080 --> 01:10:23.827
My name is (indistinct)

01:10:23.827 --> 01:10:25.560
and I am also a postdoc

01:10:25.560 --> 01:10:27.930
from Southeast Asian studies,

01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:29.367
also on South East Asian Studies.

01:10:29.367 --> 01:10:31.173
And I have a really quick question.

01:10:32.981 --> 01:10:36.240
Based on what you have
presented and new question,

01:10:36.240 --> 01:10:39.240
I'm wondering about the methodologies.

01:10:39.240 --> 01:10:42.728
About, across the chapters and authors

01:10:42.728 --> 01:10:47.728
and when they write
about their case studies.

01:10:47.810 --> 01:10:49.730
Because I think Ben mentioned that

01:10:49.730 --> 01:10:52.580
there are some case studies un-observable,

01:10:52.580 --> 01:10:54.480
and I believe that there's many cases.

01:10:55.780 --> 01:10:57.740
In terms of the documents, the recording,

01:10:57.740 --> 01:10:59.543
he says something is missing there.

01:11:01.208 --> 01:11:05.460
And then you also highlight the cases here

01:11:05.460 --> 01:11:07.190
in South Asian countries.

01:11:07.190 --> 01:11:11.580
So I'm wondering which methods could work.

01:11:11.580 --> 01:11:14.590
And in this particular cases,

01:11:14.590 --> 01:11:17.190
and in this particular area.

01:11:17.190 --> 01:11:21.530
From here to when they do
in a Holocaust, for example.

01:11:21.530 --> 01:11:26.530
Or they do other mass
violence in other places.

01:11:27.450 --> 01:11:29.900
And then how the area (indistinct)

01:11:29.900 --> 01:11:34.137
something more particularly
in the way that you authors

01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:38.813
conduct their research.

01:11:46.310 --> 01:11:48.123
- Okay, I'll be brief.

01:11:48.123 --> 01:11:51.470
The collection and the volume is by...

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:53.870
It's not only different countries,

01:11:53.870 --> 01:11:56.950
but it's also the authors
of the various chapters

01:11:56.950 --> 01:11:58.470
are coming from different disciplines.

01:11:58.470 --> 01:12:03.470
So they're using different
approaches to do the research.

01:12:04.340 --> 01:12:06.790
Sometimes with what...

01:12:06.790 --> 01:12:09.110
Although they overlap quite a bit as well.

01:12:09.110 --> 01:12:11.730
But there is interviews.

01:12:11.730 --> 01:12:13.080
Some relying more on interviews,

01:12:13.080 --> 01:12:16.323
some where on archival documents.

01:12:17.880 --> 01:12:20.550
But it does to some extent
depend on the country

01:12:20.550 --> 01:12:24.159
and what's available in
terms of the archives either.

01:12:24.159 --> 01:12:27.050
And who it's possible to interview.

01:12:27.050 --> 01:12:29.510
And that also is a factor of time.

01:12:29.510 --> 01:12:32.270
If it's something that
occurred a while ago,

01:12:32.270 --> 01:12:36.050
those people may not be
directly available to interview.

01:12:36.050 --> 01:12:37.890
Where if it's something fairly recent,

01:12:37.890 --> 01:12:40.730
you can take either
likely more interviews,

01:12:40.730 --> 01:12:43.630
but less archival resources.

01:12:43.630 --> 01:12:47.780
So I'm just going to
let Ben add onto that.

01:12:48.986 --> 01:12:50.577
- I think one of the gaps

01:12:50.577 --> 01:12:55.550
that I think we could
have filled, but didn't

01:12:55.550 --> 01:12:56.720
was East Timor.

01:12:56.720 --> 01:12:59.405
I think that's one of the
major cases of mass violence

01:12:59.405 --> 01:13:03.160
that we didn't cover.

01:13:03.160 --> 01:13:04.590
But there are others as well.

01:13:04.590 --> 01:13:08.040
We just obviously
couldn't cover everything.

01:13:08.040 --> 01:13:12.380
But on the different question

01:13:12.380 --> 01:13:17.380
of why some cases of mass
violence occur and others don't

01:13:17.700 --> 01:13:22.700
and why some regimes or
parties or individuals

01:13:23.030 --> 01:13:25.090
are more restrained than others.

01:13:25.090 --> 01:13:28.082
Come back to the question
of long-term causes

01:13:28.082 --> 01:13:29.910
of mass violence or genocide.

01:13:29.910 --> 01:13:31.990
And the immediate ones.

01:13:31.990 --> 01:13:36.084
Suddenly there's parties of institutions

01:13:36.084 --> 01:13:38.050
or leaders of institutions

01:13:38.930 --> 01:13:43.260
do make decisions that are
going to escalate violence.

01:13:43.260 --> 01:13:46.780
Jeffrey Robinson talks
about this in his chapter,

01:13:46.780 --> 01:13:49.240
quite illuminatingly.

01:13:49.240 --> 01:13:53.490
That it's not just the ethnic conflict

01:13:53.490 --> 01:13:58.490
or these clash of civilizations
as Samuel Huntington put it.

01:13:59.800 --> 01:14:04.607
Or the fact of migration or war, poverty,

01:14:06.410 --> 01:14:08.330
economic destabilization.

01:14:08.330 --> 01:14:11.430
These long-term factors can exist.

01:14:11.430 --> 01:14:16.430
But for a major crisis
to turn into a genocide

01:14:17.160 --> 01:14:20.020
or extermination or other
crimes against humanity,

01:14:20.020 --> 01:14:25.020
it still requires a decision
by a group of perpetrators

01:14:26.850 --> 01:14:29.550
to take advantage of that opportunity.

01:14:29.550 --> 01:14:34.550
To enforce or impose a
campaign of mass murder.

01:14:34.660 --> 01:14:37.950
And some leaders don't do that.

01:14:37.950 --> 01:14:40.122
They refrain from doing it.

01:14:40.122 --> 01:14:44.470
And I think they may commit violence.

01:14:44.470 --> 01:14:48.420
They may repress opposition parties.

01:14:48.420 --> 01:14:50.440
They may put people in jail,

01:14:50.440 --> 01:14:53.150
but they may not commit mass violence.

01:14:53.150 --> 01:14:57.580
Others too, and we've seen
that across the spectrum

01:14:57.580 --> 01:15:01.350
that does happen, but in
some cases it doesn't happen.

01:15:01.350 --> 01:15:04.890
And I think to look at the
immediate cause of the genocide,

01:15:04.890 --> 01:15:08.030
we have to look at
political decision-making.

01:15:08.030 --> 01:15:11.180
And sometimes that's informed by ideology.

01:15:11.180 --> 01:15:14.673
And sometimes it's informed by political

01:15:16.950 --> 01:15:21.680
or human decisions that
relate to the nature

01:15:21.680 --> 01:15:26.593
of the personality or the
group around the person.

01:15:27.450 --> 01:15:32.220
Or the group of people who decided

01:15:32.220 --> 01:15:37.220
to kill a lot of people
rather than accept less power.

01:15:38.380 --> 01:15:41.190
I think that's an
important factor as well.

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:43.903
The immediate causes of mass murder.

01:15:44.860 --> 01:15:47.203
It's political decisions.

